Z||000009||fiero56||07-07-2000||12:02 AM||||I thought about doing the work myself. I haven't had to farm out any of my projects yet. But, without a complete impact set, I don't want to work on a suspension system that has "seen it all." I watched WT III try to take off two LCAs one time. I promised myself I wouldn't even attempt that type of thing without some good air tools.||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||07-07-2000||12:28 AM||||Take that money and buy some good air tools. Once you have made the investment you could work on your fiero all you want. You could get an electric impact wrench also and cut the cost of buying an air compressor. <P>Just for the record the suspension can be disassembled and reassembled without air tools.<P>There is always renting to you know.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||lowCG||07-07-2000||01:57 AM||||I did mine after work one evening,pulled the car into the shop,right next to the lathe,yanked out the control arm bolts(air not needed),removed the old bushings without removing the control arms,measured for the ones I was soon to make from scrap UHMW plastic,installed them,and drove home a happy camper.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||07-07-2000||02:42 PM||||I called Bob at Twin Lakes today. He is out until late tonight(I'm busy)but he will be in on Saturday(I'm busy again), so I will have to try and give him a call Monday. I was hoping to take care of this Mon-Tues next week since I have those two days off. Maybe I will have some time the week after next.<P>This damn squeak is getting louder and much more frequent(passersby keep looking to see what is making all that noise). I'm at the point where I'm ready to just grab a tire iron and go to town on the car. I can't take much more of it. People who know me, know I can't stand anything not being PERFECT, and this squeak is not perfection.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Raydar||07-07-2000||03:01 PM||||56... Before you do all of that, check the metal shields behind the brake rotors. My Sonoma had a squeak that was driving me nuts. Only happened at low speeds, usually when I was driving through the parking garage at work. Really got some looks. <BR>The shields were *just* touching the ends of the control arms. Cost me $50 for someone to find it, and bend the shield about 1/4". ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||07-07-2000||09:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>I called Bob at Twin Lakes today. He is out until late tonight(I'm busy)but he will be in on Saturday(I'm busy again), so I will have to try and give him a call Monday. I was hoping to take care of this Mon-Tues next week since I have those two days off. Maybe I will have some time the week after next.<P>This damn squeak is getting louder and much more frequent(passersby keep looking to see what is making all that noise). I'm at the point where I'm ready to just grab a tire iron and go to town on the car. I can't take much more of it. People who know me, know I can't stand anything not being PERFECT, and this squeak is not perfection.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Now that I think about it, I <I>did</I> see 3 tiny specks of dust on your wing when we were at Scotts.  (For those of you that haven't seen Drews Formula, it looks like it just came off of the showroom floor.)<P><P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000015||Old Lar||07-07-2000||09:47 PM||||Bob did the bushings on my 87, will be doing the bushings on my 88 (one of his 3.4 conversions in process).  Bob, like Ed is a good mechanic and is more concerned about doing it right, which is what I want. The bushings ar a lot of money for a hunk of plastic.  Lots of labor time to do it right.  Mr. Goodwrenche's eyes rolled back into his head when I asked about doing that job on my Fiero.  MY pocket would have been made real light.<P>I neither have the time, skill, tools, space or patience to experiment in replacing bushings engines, transmissions etc.  <P>Reading about all the engine swaps that people want to try is scary to me.  Geeze I've got a 219 cu inch straight 6 from a 47 Chevy, would that be an easy swap into a Fiero.  After all its a GM engine, wouldn't it bolt right in to a getrag 5 speed?<P>Bob's a busy guy, catch him early, he's usually at his shop by 0700 central time zone.||||reg||1
Z||000016||fiero56||07-07-2000||11:38 PM||||If he is usually in his shop around 7, I might be able to catch him in time(8am here). <P>I have performed two clutch changes(dropping engine, removing tranny, etc) and all sorts of "big" projects, but suspensions are not something I know much about, so I prefer to leave them to those who know what they are doing.||||reg||1
Z||000017||fiero56||07-08-2000||08:40 AM||||I just got off the phone with Bob. He sounds like a very nice guy, and he know his stuff too. But, the labor price he quoted me is more than double what Ed's average charge with alignment is(I payed just a little more for my Formula than what Bob quoted). <P>I think I will just make the drive down to Ed Parks one of these days and have THE FIERO GENIUS work on my car. I have never had a bad experience with Ed, he is one of the greatest people I have ever met.||||reg||1
Z||000018||BN Boomer||07-08-2000||09:11 AM||||I noticed that you are complaining about a squeak in your current suspension bushings and want to install new polyurethane bushings. Just ouf curiosity, don't the polyurethane bushings squeak even more than rubber ones? If squeaks bother you, you might be really unhappy with the results of this upgrade.||||reg||1
